I also want to start out by going back to process. As you heard when we worked this bill in committee, we had no idea that the bathroom bill balloon amendment was going to be brought.

01:56:25.100 --> 01:56:28.100
It never had a public hearing.

01:56:28.100 --> 01:56:35.100
We hardly had any time to read or analyze how it impacted the bill.

01:56:35.100 --> 01:56:43.100
But most importantly, a question was never answered. And that question is, how is this possibly germane to the underlying bill?

01:56:44.100 --> 01:56:52.100
When I asked that question in committee, the chair graphs for a response and I supplied the answer.

01:56:52.100 --> 01:56:58.100
And that answer is that these are connected because they both discriminate against trans people.

01:56:58.100 --> 01:57:04.100
They are both based on ignorance, fear, mongering and misinformation.

01:57:04.100 --> 01:57:16.100
There is no actual problem that these bills solve. There is no existing harm that needs to be addressed.

01:57:16.100 --> 01:57:23.100
And I'm going to give some examples of that, some of which have already been mentioned.

01:57:23.100 --> 01:57:39.100
As was outlined with the court of appeals case, there is absolutely no evidence of any harm whatsoever that was provided to prove that allowing someone to change the gender marker on their driver's license or birth certificate.

01:57:39.100 --> 01:57:49.100
In any way, impacts the ability of law enforcement to do their job or people to interact with government.

01:57:49.100 --> 01:57:54.100
And this is a quote from the court of appeals decision.

01:57:55.100 --> 01:58:07.100
They found that no one was able to bring forward any instance of any feared harm of misidentification of criminals in the last 16 years or even the potential that it could be a problem.

01:58:07.100 --> 01:58:11.100
Instead, the evidence was overwhelming that there was no harm.

01:58:11.100 --> 01:58:24.100
So in the 16 years that gender marker changes have been allowed, no harm has ever been identified by a law enforcement officer or anyone in the state that allowing gender marker changes has caused.

01:58:24.100 --> 01:58:34.100
And I also want to highlight testimony that was provided by a Johnson County lieutenant from the Johnson County Sheriff's Office as part of this Court of Appeals decision.

01:58:34.100 --> 01:58:49.100
He testified that in his 23 years of employment, he had not been made aware of any officer that had a problem with transgender person and their driver's license ever in his 23 years of working in the Johnson County Sheriff's Office.

01:58:49.100 --> 01:59:03.100
This has been happening for at least 16 years, if not longer, and there are have been no issues identified with allowing people to change their gender markers on their driver's license and birth certificates.

01:59:03.100 --> 01:59:16.100
In regards to the bathroom bill portion of this massive bill, I also want to highlight that research shows there is no link between trans inclusive bathrooms and crimes in bathrooms or locker rooms.

01:59:16.100 --> 01:59:23.100
In fact, non discrimination laws protecting transgender people in these kinds of facilities have been around for a very long time.

01:59:24.100 --> 01:59:27.100
In fact, since the Civil Rights Act of 1964.

01:59:27.100 --> 01:59:33.100
And in that time, there has been no increase in public safety incidents in restrooms or locker rooms.

01:59:33.100 --> 01:59:36.100
There is no problem. There's nothing that needs to be solved.

01:59:37.100 --> 01:59:53.100
But what these bills do is cause real harm to transgender intersects and gender diverse people.

01:59:53.100 --> 02:00:08.100
The ignorance misinformation and disinformation underlying the bill feed into hateful stereotypes that create real violence with showing your ID, for example, it should not be a dangerous act, but this bill would make it so.

02:00:08.100 --> 02:00:13.100
It will force trans people to out themselves in multiple aspects of public life.

02:00:13.100 --> 02:00:20.100
They already face extensive discrimination and hate based violence, just for being themselves.

02:00:20.100 --> 02:00:30.100
If you think about the instances in which you need a legal document, a public ID, you need them to vote to get a job to go to school, even to get a library card.

02:00:30.100 --> 02:00:40.100
In laws that put transgender people at a more risk of hate based violence for engaging in everyday actions like this would because we would be outing them in these various aspects of public life.

02:00:40.100 --> 02:00:47.100
It means that transgender canons could miss out on those important parts of their life in order to protect their personal safety.

02:00:47.100 --> 02:01:02.100
Bills like this literally intimidate trans people out of living their full lives because they are afraid of the danger in the violence that could potentially befall them.

02:01:02.100 --> 02:01:08.100
In regards to the bathroom provision of this bill.

02:01:08.100 --> 02:01:19.100
Studies have found that discriminatory bathroom and locker room policies are associated with higher rates of violence and assault towards trans people, not other people using the bathroom.

02:01:19.100 --> 02:01:23.100
This is what creates violence for trans people in those public spaces.

02:01:32.100 --> 02:01:43.100
There is no problem that this bill solves, but there is harm that it will cause.

02:01:43.100 --> 02:01:52.100
Trans people just want to be left alone.

02:01:52.100 --> 02:01:54.100
They just want to live their lives.

02:01:54.100 --> 02:01:58.100
They want to live their lives free from government interference.

02:01:58.100 --> 02:02:05.100
They want to have an ID that reflects their gender, their presentation.

02:02:05.100 --> 02:02:11.100
As we heard in committee, they want an identification that is an artifact of their expression.

02:02:11.100 --> 02:02:14.100
And you are taking that away with this bill.

02:02:14.100 --> 02:02:22.100
Trans people just want to pee in peace like the rest of us.

02:02:22.100 --> 02:02:41.100
And with this bill, you are taking the ability of them to do that away.

02:02:41.100 --> 02:02:50.100
So, I want to highlight one additional thing here.

02:02:50.100 --> 02:03:01.100
I apologize, we did not have a ton of time to get ready, so I am organizing my thoughts as I am speaking.

02:03:01.100 --> 02:03:12.100
The other side of this, it is not just discrimination in public spaces, it's not just discrimination of allowing people to have driver's licenses that reflect their gender identities.

02:03:12.100 --> 02:03:24.100
I don't know how closely you have read the bathroom bill, but it is also taking away spaces that are actively created to be inclusive for transgender diverse and intersex people.

02:03:24.100 --> 02:03:37.100
So in the bathroom bill portion of this bill, it is making it so that gender neutral multi occupancy spaces are illegal.

02:03:37.100 --> 02:03:52.100
So bathrooms that are gender neutral that have multiple stalls changing rooms that are designed to be gender neutral spaces that are intended to be gender gender neutral other than a single occupancy stall are illegal under this bill.

02:03:52.100 --> 02:03:56.100
Kansas City International Airport bathrooms would be illegal.

02:03:56.100 --> 02:04:01.100
The bathrooms at my favorite local brewery would be illegal.

02:04:01.100 --> 02:04:12.100
The bathrooms that I know are in some of our local K through 12 schools to support and be inclusive that have multiple gender neutral stalls will be illegal.

02:04:12.100 --> 02:04:21.100
And that is cruel. It is not just discrimination. This bill takes away spaces that are actively created to be inclusive for people who are gender diverse.

02:04:21.100 --> 02:04:36.100
And I think it is important to talk about that.

02:04:37.100 --> 02:04:42.100
This bill does allow for single occupancy gender neutral spaces. It does not require them.

02:04:42.100 --> 02:04:51.100
And I think if we're going to take the ability away for trans people to use the bathroom and public spaces that that should be required. So I have an amendment.

02:05:07.100 --> 02:05:14.100
The amendment appears short. The clerk will read.

02:05:14.100 --> 02:05:24.100
I move to amend House substitute for SB 244 on page two in line 16 after the period by inserting the governing body or the chief administrative officer.

02:05:25.100 --> 02:05:39.100
If no governing body exists of each public building shall establish a single occupancy restroom is described in subsection a 7 a on each floor of such public building, including any structure owned by such governmental entity that contains a restroom.

02:05:39.100 --> 02:05:47.100
In line 22 by striking a and inserting additional also in line 22 by striking space and inserting spaces.

02:05:47.100 --> 02:05:51.100
Representative, do you wish to explain your amendment?

02:05:52.100 --> 02:06:00.100
Yes, thank you, Mr. Chair. So this amendment essentially does what I described. It is specific to restrooms. But.

02:06:00.100 --> 02:06:13.100
If this were to go into the bill, then it would require that every public building shall establish a single occupancy gender neutral restroom on each floor of the public building so that.

02:06:14.100 --> 02:06:27.100
People regardless of their gender identity sexual orientation or for any other reason people who may want to use a single occupancy bathroom will have access to one on every single floor of a public building.

02:06:27.100 --> 02:06:40.100
And with that, I stand for questions.

02:06:40.100 --> 02:06:44.100
Does anybody wish to speak to the amendment?

02:06:44.100 --> 02:06:48.100
The chair recognizes representative Humphreys.

02:06:49.100 --> 02:06:57.100
Could I ask a quick question of the bringer of the amendment?

02:06:57.100 --> 02:07:01.100
Did you get a fiscal note for this amendment?

02:07:01.100 --> 02:07:06.100
Is there a fiscal note for the underlying bill? Is that a no?

02:07:06.100 --> 02:07:10.100
That was a question. Okay. Thank you.

02:07:11.100 --> 02:07:19.100
Body, what I would like to point out is this amendment requires construction and building.

02:07:19.100 --> 02:07:33.100
The fiscal note would be, I mean, I don't even know what this will do, but it requires Kansas or sorry, government buildings to build or provide single space restrooms.

02:07:34.100 --> 02:07:41.100
The other thing is, there's no enforcement in this amendment.

02:07:41.100 --> 02:07:56.100
So how would that work? What would happen there? So, of course, I'm asking you to vote this amendment down because I also want to point out the underlying bill, there is no requirement that anyone build anything.

02:07:57.100 --> 02:08:12.100
There's not really, I mean, think about it, what might the fiscal note be changing the labels on doors, maybe, but the amendment would certainly bring a fiscal burden to the municipality.

02:08:12.100 --> 02:08:16.100
So I ask you to vote no.

02:08:16.100 --> 02:08:26.100
And then the other member wish to speak to the amendment. The chair recognizes representative Haskins.

02:08:26.100 --> 02:08:33.100
Just checking in on you gave you that option a while back. Here I am again.

02:08:33.100 --> 02:08:41.100
One more chance got the amendment. Now, I do find it interesting that fiscal note is now really important.

02:08:41.100 --> 02:08:48.100
And that's a question here and that we can laugh at that because it's really not that funny that we don't know how things are going to cost.

02:08:48.100 --> 02:08:57.100
So, but because we don't have a fiscal note for the first one, the argument that we should have a fiscal note for this has already been negated because we don't think it's important.

02:08:57.100 --> 02:09:11.100
I want you to also listen to what the amendment is asking. The amendment is actually asking for us to be able to have the quality of life that all Kansans deserve and that they should be able to have.

02:09:11.100 --> 02:09:20.100
Now, the whole signage thing or how much it's going to cost. Are you kidding me? The bill itself can't even justify how much it's going to cost.

02:09:20.100 --> 02:09:26.100
How are we going to enforce it? Well, I guarantee you will probably have some vigilantes in the restrooms.

02:09:26.100 --> 02:09:32.100
So, how are we going to force it? That doesn't seem to be a unique argument against it.

02:09:32.100 --> 02:09:39.100
Folks, let's do what's right for Kansas. Let's support the amendment and let's move on. Thank you, Mr. Chair.

02:09:40.100 --> 02:09:46.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the amendment? The Chair recognizes Representative Ballard.

02:09:53.100 --> 02:09:57.100
Thank you very much.

02:09:57.100 --> 02:10:05.100
Boy, I said there a long time. My father, this one, I have to say.

02:10:05.100 --> 02:10:12.100
We all have to go to the bathroom. Some of us wait a long time and we're running to get there.

02:10:12.100 --> 02:10:28.100
If you're going to limit the bathrooms, you got to at least provide one. I mean, that's just not just human. That's a necessary needed place.

02:10:29.100 --> 02:10:37.100
We laugh and it's kind of funny, except it's not really. If you've had to go to the bathroom in a hurry and you don't have a place to go, you panic.

02:10:37.100 --> 02:10:42.100
So, I don't understand why this should be a problem.

02:10:42.100 --> 02:10:47.100
You know, and when I'm at it, appropriations, we're cutting.

02:10:47.100 --> 02:10:54.100
We have to find so much money to cut, but yet we're spending money and no one is really addressing that.

02:10:55.100 --> 02:11:04.100
And all my years of walking on doors and I have not on a few hundred doors. No one has ever asked me about bathrooms.

02:11:04.100 --> 02:11:09.100
They've asked me about a lot of other things, but not about bathrooms.

02:11:09.100 --> 02:11:15.100
I've been offered, if I've been out there a long time, would I like to come inside and go to the bathroom?

02:11:15.100 --> 02:11:19.100
And a couple of times I said, yes, thank you very much.

02:11:20.100 --> 02:11:26.100
But you know, I'm making light of it, but boy, I tell you, my blood pressure is up.

02:11:26.100 --> 02:11:32.100
So, I would look at this in terms of bathroom choices over the years. I have addressed those many times.

02:11:32.100 --> 02:11:41.100
I cannot and I will not support any discrimination against another human being.

02:11:42.100 --> 02:11:49.100
Anyway, shape or form. And for those of you that know me, you know, I stay true to that.

02:11:49.100 --> 02:11:54.100
I would protect any of you if anybody was discriminating against you.

02:11:54.100 --> 02:12:01.100
But when we get down to bathrooms, that even should not be an issue.

02:12:01.100 --> 02:12:08.100
So, I would simply say to you, if this was really a big issue,

02:12:08.100 --> 02:12:13.100
I would have allowed people in Kansas to weigh in on it.

02:12:13.100 --> 02:12:15.100
And all the years they've never mentioned it.

02:12:15.100 --> 02:12:18.100
Representative, could you please stay on the motion to amend, please?

02:12:18.100 --> 02:12:23.100
Yes, thank you. Thank you for keeping me on track because I was really getting upset.

02:12:23.100 --> 02:12:30.100
But I would really say to you, bathrooms seriously,

02:12:30.100 --> 02:12:35.100
if you're going to take it away from them on one place,

02:12:35.100 --> 02:12:40.100
do the right thing and allow one to be someplace else.

02:12:40.100 --> 02:12:46.100
For that reason, I support this amendment because it's the human thing to do,

02:12:46.100 --> 02:12:49.100
but it's also the legal thing to do.

02:12:49.100 --> 02:12:54.100
And you think of yourself when you have to go, you have to go.

02:12:54.100 --> 02:12:57.100
Thank you very much, Mr. Chair.

02:12:57.100 --> 02:13:00.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the amendment?

02:13:00.100 --> 02:13:03.100
Seeing none, Representative, you may close on your amendment.

02:13:05.100 --> 02:13:07.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair.

02:13:07.100 --> 02:13:13.100
Practically speaking, single occupancy bathrooms are often unavailable, inconvenient,

02:13:13.100 --> 02:13:16.100
or inaccessible.

02:13:16.100 --> 02:13:21.100
And as a government, if this legislature is going to force transgender people

02:13:21.100 --> 02:13:24.100
to use private bathrooms simply because they're trans,

02:13:24.100 --> 02:13:31.100
we sure as heck better have one available on every single floor of every single government building.

02:13:32.100 --> 02:13:34.100
And that is why I brought this amendment.

02:13:34.100 --> 02:13:36.100
So I move my amendment.

02:13:36.100 --> 02:13:39.100
You've heard the motion by Representative on to amend all those in favor,

02:13:39.100 --> 02:13:43.100
signified by saying aye.

02:13:43.100 --> 02:13:46.100
All opposed, nay.

02:13:46.100 --> 02:13:48.100
Nose appear to have it.

02:13:48.100 --> 02:13:49.100
Division has been called.

02:13:49.100 --> 02:13:58.100
The clerk will open the roll and you may vote.

02:13:58.100 --> 02:14:05.100
Not seeing 15 hands, roll call is not in order.

02:14:05.100 --> 02:14:08.100
Seeing 15 hands, a roll call is now in order.

02:14:29.100 --> 02:14:34.100
Is every member have the opportunity to vote?

02:14:34.100 --> 02:14:38.100
The clerk will close the roll and take the tally.

02:14:38.100 --> 02:14:46.100
Does any member wish to change their vote?

02:14:46.100 --> 02:14:51.100
Representative Schlingen-Sleepen votes aye.

02:14:52.100 --> 02:14:57.100
Any other?

02:14:57.100 --> 02:15:04.100
Representative Anderson votes no.

02:15:04.100 --> 02:15:08.100
Representative Van Houden votes no.

02:15:08.100 --> 02:15:13.100
And Representative Kessler votes no.

02:15:13.100 --> 02:15:18.100
Representative Essex votes no.

02:15:18.100 --> 02:15:24.100
Any other changes of vote?

02:15:24.100 --> 02:15:30.100
Seeing none, the clerk will close the roll and take the tally.

02:15:30.100 --> 02:15:34.100
36 having voted in favor and 85 against the motion fails.

02:15:34.100 --> 02:15:40.100
Representative Vaughn used to have the floor.

02:15:40.100 --> 02:15:41.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair.

02:15:41.100 --> 02:15:47.100
I think the failure of that amendment proves what this bill is really about.

02:15:48.100 --> 02:15:50.100
It's not about clarifying anything.

02:15:50.100 --> 02:15:57.100
It's not about statutory alignment or whatever.

02:15:57.100 --> 02:16:00.100
This is about discriminating against trans people.

02:16:00.100 --> 02:16:05.100
This is about making it impossible for them to use public spaces.

02:16:05.100 --> 02:16:10.100
And if we can't even make a simple accommodation, like ensuring there's a bathroom that they

02:16:10.100 --> 02:16:16.100
could use on every single floor of a government building, I think that proves my point.

02:16:16.100 --> 02:16:22.100
The reality is that trans people are significantly more likely to be victims of violent crime

02:16:22.100 --> 02:16:28.100
than the rest of the population over four times more likely per recent research.

02:16:28.100 --> 02:16:31.100
They are the ones who will be harmed by this policy.

02:16:31.100 --> 02:16:39.100
They are the ones who are harmed by the policies that this legislature keeps passing to take away their rights.

02:16:39.100 --> 02:16:43.100
And I just want to highlight that because here we are again.

02:16:44.100 --> 02:16:49.100
First, this legislature took away trans kids' ability to play sports.

02:16:49.100 --> 02:16:52.100
Then it took away their ability to access health care.

02:16:52.100 --> 02:17:00.100
Now you are taking away trans-cansons' abilities to be in and use public spaces.

02:17:00.100 --> 02:17:05.100
You are taking away their identities, their identities on their driver's license and their birth certificate.

02:17:05.100 --> 02:17:09.100
And most importantly and most shamefully, you are taking away their voice.

02:17:09.100 --> 02:17:15.100
You are taking away their ability to voice opposition on this bill and how terrible it is.

02:17:15.100 --> 02:17:19.100
And to that I say shame on you.

02:17:19.100 --> 02:17:21.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the bill?

02:17:21.100 --> 02:17:36.100
The chair recognizes Representative McDonald.

02:17:36.100 --> 02:17:38.100
Thank you Mr. Chair.

02:17:38.100 --> 02:17:44.100
I really appreciate the opportunity to get up and visit with you all a little bit more about this bill.

02:17:44.100 --> 02:17:51.100
In legislative modernization committee this morning we had a really good conversation about testimony.

02:17:51.100 --> 02:18:00.100
How we involve the public in being able to weigh in and make informed decisions to inform our decisions.

02:18:00.100 --> 02:18:09.100
We talked about ways that we get in the way of the public being able to testify in a convenient and understandable way.

02:18:09.100 --> 02:18:13.100
We talked about ways we could be more transparent.

02:18:13.100 --> 02:18:19.100
We talked about ways we inadvertently keep them out of the loop.

02:18:19.100 --> 02:18:24.100
We talked about ways that we could make things better here.

02:18:24.100 --> 02:18:28.100
And this my friends is not the way we do it.

02:18:28.100 --> 02:18:31.100
Again we are rushing through.

02:18:31.100 --> 02:18:35.100
We could have taken the time that this sort of debate deserves.

02:18:35.100 --> 02:18:40.100
And I would argue we're going to take the time today whether we all like it or not.

02:18:40.100 --> 02:18:46.100
But in committee we could have really taken the time to sit down to listen to each other for understanding.

02:18:46.100 --> 02:18:48.100
Not for rebuttal.

02:18:48.100 --> 02:18:54.100
Not to score campaign points or get big donors to give us some attention.

02:18:54.100 --> 02:18:58.100
Not because we have a convention coming up this weekend and we need something to talk about.

02:18:58.100 --> 02:19:00.100
We need to come with a win.

02:19:00.100 --> 02:19:11.100
But really because we want to sit down with each other in a spirit of cooperation finding real solutions to problems that real Kansans are facing.

02:19:11.100 --> 02:19:19.100
I read somewhere that this driver's license drama really only impacts about 500 Kansans.

02:19:19.100 --> 02:19:29.100
I started to think a little bit about how much time and money we've spent on this topic in this building over the last few years.

02:19:29.100 --> 02:19:38.100
My friend from Olathe mentioned earlier that this has been really argued years and years before.

02:19:38.100 --> 02:19:43.100
And I kind of find that a little insulting because not all of us were here years before.

02:19:43.100 --> 02:19:50.100
The conversations about bathroom bills and sports and even healthcare decisions.

02:19:50.100 --> 02:20:00.100
Those things deserve a real conversation with the people here today who will be making these decisions.

02:20:00.100 --> 02:20:06.100
Now while some of you have been here a long time, I really do appreciate that institutional knowledge that you bring.

02:20:06.100 --> 02:20:12.100
But when you dismiss those of us who have not been here very long, when you roll your eyes and say

02:20:12.100 --> 02:20:15.100
we already hashed this out years ago.

02:20:15.100 --> 02:20:18.100
This is old news.

02:20:18.100 --> 02:20:20.100
Times change.

02:20:20.100 --> 02:20:22.100
Clearly people change.

02:20:22.100 --> 02:20:27.100
People come in and out of this building and there have been many people sitting in your chair before you.

02:20:27.100 --> 02:20:31.100
And there will be many people God willing sitting in your chair after you leave.

02:20:31.100 --> 02:20:39.100
The decisions we make here, the processes we make, we put into place, they're important.

02:20:39.100 --> 02:20:43.100
And cutting the public out in the way that you have here today.

02:20:43.100 --> 02:20:47.100
I would argue it's really unethical and unprofessional.

02:20:47.100 --> 02:20:49.100
Reeks of a lack of transparency.

02:20:49.100 --> 02:20:54.100
Seems to me kind of desperate.

02:20:54.100 --> 02:21:00.100
When you feel like you can't let the public weigh in and you just need to shove it through.

02:21:00.100 --> 02:21:07.100
I realize we're going to have a blue wave this year and I can understand why some of you might want to double down

02:21:07.100 --> 02:21:11.100
and triple down on past campaign promises.

02:21:11.100 --> 02:21:18.100
This may seem like a Hail Mary to get you a little bit more momentum going into campaign season.

02:21:18.100 --> 02:21:27.100
But recycling this feeds division, fear, polarization.

02:21:27.100 --> 02:21:34.100
When we could be seriously coming together and solving some real problems for real Kansans.

02:21:35.100 --> 02:21:40.100
When I knock doors in really nice neighborhoods, I have people confiding in me.

02:21:40.100 --> 02:21:43.100
I cannot afford my kids soccer this year.

02:21:43.100 --> 02:21:46.100
My parents just moved back in with me.

02:21:46.100 --> 02:21:49.100
I can't afford to put them in a nursing home.

02:21:49.100 --> 02:21:52.100
I can't afford the care they need each day while I'm at work.

02:21:52.100 --> 02:21:58.100
My wife can't work because we've got to pay for child care and she doesn't make enough to cover child care.

02:21:59.100 --> 02:22:06.100
These are real issues that we could be focusing on because we have been given that power by the people who sent us here.

02:22:06.100 --> 02:22:12.100
Yet today we're banging the same old drum.

02:22:12.100 --> 02:22:20.100
We say with one out of one side of our mouth, we care about financial decisions that we make for taxpayers with their money.

02:22:20.100 --> 02:22:26.100
Yet we can shove something like this through without a fiscal note.

02:22:26.100 --> 02:22:32.100
The carry of the bill mentioned something about how there's no infrastructure that has to be changed.

02:22:32.100 --> 02:22:34.100
That's not the way I read it.

02:22:34.100 --> 02:22:38.100
And when I've talked to attorneys in my caucus, that's most certainly not the way they read it.

02:22:38.100 --> 02:22:45.100
If you intended to not have to do any construction, why was that not written into this bill?

02:22:45.100 --> 02:22:50.100
Why are you all not bringing an amendment to say no construction necessary?

02:22:51.100 --> 02:22:58.100
If it's as simple as sticking a piece of paper on a door on a government building, policies can do that.

02:22:58.100 --> 02:23:03.100
Do we really need a statute to do that? We do not.

02:23:03.100 --> 02:23:07.100
And by the way, why can't we pass clean bills in this building?

02:23:07.100 --> 02:23:17.100
There is no need for us to be bundling some driver's license law because Chris Cobuck has batted his job into some rehashing of a bathroom bill.

02:23:18.100 --> 02:23:26.100
I don't really understand why we need to be putting two divisive issues in one bundle without giving the public a proper chance to vet it,

02:23:26.100 --> 02:23:31.100
shoving it through so that in a month's time, there's so much ambiguity here.

02:23:31.100 --> 02:23:38.100
How are jurisdictions going to figure out exactly what you mean other than through a lawsuit?

02:23:38.100 --> 02:23:44.100
In Olayza School District, you know how many buildings the school district owns?

02:23:44.100 --> 02:23:47.100
58 buildings in my Olayza School District.

02:23:47.100 --> 02:23:51.100
I also represent Blue Valley School District. Do you know how many buildings they own?

02:23:51.100 --> 02:23:54.100
39 buildings.

02:23:54.100 --> 02:23:59.100
Maybe some of you represent Shawnee Mission School District, 51 buildings.

02:23:59.100 --> 02:24:04.100
The Soto School District, 14 buildings and growing, by the way.

02:24:04.100 --> 02:24:08.100
Gardner, 11 school buildings, Spring Hill, 13 school buildings.

02:24:08.100 --> 02:24:12.100
That's just some of the districts in Johnson County.

02:24:12.100 --> 02:24:18.100
Now, I know that a lot of you feel like in Johnson County, we live in the land of plenty where money is, you know, no big deal,

02:24:18.100 --> 02:24:23.100
but that's not our reality in our school districts.

02:24:23.100 --> 02:24:28.100
We have to pass bonds to build new buildings.

02:24:28.100 --> 02:24:32.100
When we build new buildings, we try to do so with the latest infrastructure in mind.

02:24:32.100 --> 02:24:38.100
We try to make sure that we're using the best practices, like at the Kansas City International Airport,

02:24:38.100 --> 02:24:47.100
where they have one bank of bathrooms, private stalls, but it's coed, because it doesn't matter when you shut the door.

02:24:47.100 --> 02:24:53.100
Nobody needs to be in your pants or in your business of what's in your pants.

02:24:53.100 --> 02:25:01.100
Now, my friend from Manhattan, she mentioned earlier that we want to make sure and do no harm.

02:25:01.100 --> 02:25:08.100
She said that this bill is important because of safety, that we owe everyone dignity.

02:25:08.100 --> 02:25:15.100
Emotional security is really important, that restrooms are not political spaces.

02:25:15.100 --> 02:25:17.100
They're private spaces.

02:25:17.100 --> 02:25:20.100
And for those of you who aren't listening, let me repeat myself.

02:25:20.100 --> 02:25:25.100
My friend from Manhattan said, we should do no harm.

02:25:25.100 --> 02:25:27.100
Safety is important.

02:25:27.100 --> 02:25:28.100
Dignity.

02:25:28.100 --> 02:25:30.100
Emotional security.

02:25:30.100 --> 02:25:34.100
Restrooms are not political spaces.

02:25:34.100 --> 02:25:39.100
They are private spaces.

02:25:39.100 --> 02:25:43.100
They're private for all of us.

02:25:43.100 --> 02:25:51.100
It is hard enough for transgender teenage girls to face extra stigma.

02:25:51.100 --> 02:26:00.100
My friend from Manhattan is a nurse, a school nurse, and she has talked about how she has helped young women going through their first mental cycles.

02:26:00.100 --> 02:26:01.100
Imagine this.

02:26:01.100 --> 02:26:06.100
We have denied hormone blockers for children who are struggling with their gender identity.

02:26:06.100 --> 02:26:15.100
So not only are they struggling with normal puberty, they aren't even able to delay puberty long enough to figure out what's happening with their bodies.

02:26:15.100 --> 02:26:17.100
We've denied them health care.

02:26:17.100 --> 02:26:29.100
We're putting them through more trauma, hurting their dignity, refusing to give them a private space to just go to the bathroom.

02:26:29.100 --> 02:26:38.100
Imagine your body revolting and menstruating when you feel like that is a foreign thing when you don't feel like you fit in your body.

02:26:38.100 --> 02:26:44.100
And we as adults are coming in, traumatizing these children further.

02:26:44.100 --> 02:26:50.100
Disregarding their safety, their dignity, their emotional security.

02:26:50.100 --> 02:26:53.100
We are doing them harm.

02:26:53.100 --> 02:26:59.100
Again, like my friend from Manhattan said, restrooms are not political spaces.

02:26:59.100 --> 02:27:03.100
They are private spaces.

02:27:03.100 --> 02:27:12.100
Other friends have already mentioned the increased prevalence of assaults on transgender individuals.

02:27:12.100 --> 02:27:21.100
And I remember someone early in this conversation mentioned trauma that women go through when women are attacked.

02:27:21.100 --> 02:27:27.100
Imagine being attacked in a sexual way.

02:27:27.100 --> 02:27:30.100
Whether you're transgender or not, it's trauma.

02:27:30.100 --> 02:27:40.100
And when you put trans people in a space where they stand out, they're inviting, it is inviting, abuse and trauma.

02:27:40.100 --> 02:27:46.100
Now let's talk a little bit about education. Can we do that?

02:27:46.100 --> 02:27:55.100
I'll wait.

02:27:55.100 --> 02:27:57.100
Seems like private conversations are...

02:27:57.100 --> 02:27:58.100
Representative, please continue.

02:27:58.100 --> 02:28:02.100
I'd love for people to hear my voice.

02:28:02.100 --> 02:28:08.100
My friends in the education community, we all want our kids to get a good education, correct?

02:28:08.100 --> 02:28:12.100
We talk a lot about dollars need to go to the classroom, right?

02:28:12.100 --> 02:28:14.100
That's what I hear all the time.

02:28:14.100 --> 02:28:25.100
Why are we spending money renovating bathrooms in school districts and school buildings to solve a problem that does not really exist?

02:28:25.100 --> 02:28:33.100
I have a son who has long hair and he made a decision when he was about seven years old, but he was going to have long hair.

02:28:33.100 --> 02:28:35.100
And I thought, you know what, he's seven.

02:28:35.100 --> 02:28:40.100
Let's let him grow his hair out while he's young, and then when he's a grown up, I can have him be a clean-cut guy.

02:28:40.100 --> 02:28:45.100
It just didn't work out that way. That long hair has become part of his identity.

02:28:45.100 --> 02:28:49.100
But he has the misfortune of looking exactly like me.

02:28:49.100 --> 02:28:57.100
And before he could grow a full beard of facial hair, very, very often, when we would go out to dinner, he would be misgendered.

02:28:57.100 --> 02:29:01.100
People would say, what can I get you ladies to drink? What can I get you ladies to eat?

02:29:01.100 --> 02:29:06.100
And he didn't worry much about it because he could see how someone might misinterpret his gender.

02:29:06.100 --> 02:29:11.100
But as a seven-year-old, an eight-year-old, a ten-year-old, he didn't really think much about it.

02:29:11.100 --> 02:29:19.100
My worry as a mom, when you all started passing these bills about bathrooms, is that my son would go into the bathroom,

02:29:19.100 --> 02:29:24.100
and a grown man would say, what are you doing in here, little girl?

02:29:24.100 --> 02:29:31.100
It proved to me that you're a boy. What is the fixation with what's in people's pants?

02:29:31.100 --> 02:29:37.100
It is creepy that people focus so much on what is in children's pants.

02:29:37.100 --> 02:29:47.100
Embarrassing. It's an insult to this room. It's an insult to this body.

02:29:47.100 --> 02:29:53.100
I'd like to go back for a minute to my concern about construction.

02:29:53.100 --> 02:30:01.100
There is no fiscal note, and there is no allotment of money that's been given to this effort.

02:30:01.100 --> 02:30:08.100
And although I know that the carrier of the bill continues to say there will be no construction involved,

02:30:08.100 --> 02:30:14.100
forgive me, but I just don't see how that's possible, so I have an amendment.

02:30:23.100 --> 02:30:30.100
The amendment appears short. The clerk will read.

02:30:30.100 --> 02:30:37.100
I move to a Menhouse substitute for SB244 on page two, following line 22 by inserting three.

02:30:37.100 --> 02:30:42.100
A governmental entity shall have four years from the effective date of this act to complete any remodeling

02:30:42.100 --> 02:30:49.100
or reconstruction of any public building owned by such governmental entity that is necessary to comply with the provisions of the subsection.

02:30:49.100 --> 02:30:58.100
Government entities shall not be in violation of this section with respect to any such public building during such four year period.

02:30:58.100 --> 02:31:03.100
Representative, do you wish to explain your amendment? Yes, sir. I think I do.

02:31:03.100 --> 02:31:10.100
So my stepdad was in construction, and I know that nothing in construction happens overnight,

02:31:10.100 --> 02:31:16.100
and it's become pretty clear that whatever we do today, it's going to be enacted in about a month.

02:31:16.100 --> 02:31:22.100
I don't know if you guys have had any construction done lightly, but it's very hard to get anybody to even come give a bid,

02:31:22.100 --> 02:31:27.100
much less show up and do the work. So I think it's only fair if we're going to put this upon our municipalities,

02:31:27.100 --> 02:31:35.100
our school districts, our community colleges, our cities, our colleges that we need to give them some time.

02:31:35.100 --> 02:31:44.100
So my amendment would just give all government entities four years from the date of publish to complete any remodeling

02:31:44.100 --> 02:31:48.100
or reconstruction of any building that's owned by the government.

02:31:48.100 --> 02:31:52.100
And I think this is necessary because in our country right now,

02:31:52.100 --> 02:31:56.100
we've really been cracking down a lot on immigration.

02:31:56.100 --> 02:32:02.100
And I've noticed that a lot of my friends who are builders are having a harder time staffing their crews.

02:32:02.100 --> 02:32:08.100
So that's just going to add to more trouble as far as trying to get this construction done in a timely fashion.

02:32:08.100 --> 02:32:12.100
I know that in Johnson County, we have a lot of people there in the construction industry,

02:32:12.100 --> 02:32:18.100
but I can only imagine when you live in a small town, it might be hard for you to get all of the trades involved,

02:32:18.100 --> 02:32:26.100
the plumbing, the electrician, the tile workers, all of that to show up and get your school bathroom remodeled in a month.

02:32:26.100 --> 02:32:32.100
So I'd like for us to give our municipalities a four-year window to get this construction done,

02:32:32.100 --> 02:32:36.100
make sure that any litigation that happens surely in four years, it'll be all played out.

02:32:36.100 --> 02:32:42.100
But this won't be an immediate cost on any existing buildings owned by the government.

02:32:42.100 --> 02:32:44.100
Happy to stand for questions.

02:32:44.100 --> 02:32:50.100
Does any member wish to speak to the amendment? The chair recognizes Representative Humphreys.

02:32:58.100 --> 02:33:02.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair. I'm a little perplexed by the amendment,

02:33:02.100 --> 02:33:08.100
because I just want to read from the bill that says that this subsection,

02:33:08.100 --> 02:33:12.100
which is amended here,

02:33:12.100 --> 02:33:20.100
this subsection shall not be construed to prohibit a governing body or chief administrative officer

02:33:20.100 --> 02:33:24.100
from establishing a single occupancy private space.

02:33:24.100 --> 02:33:30.100
So the bill says if an entity wants to do a single occupancy private space,

02:33:30.100 --> 02:33:34.100
they may, that would be totally their choice.

02:33:34.100 --> 02:33:42.100
This bill has nothing in it that says that they must comply that they have to do it.

02:33:42.100 --> 02:33:50.100
And the amendment says that they would have four years to comply with the provisions of this subsection.

02:33:50.100 --> 02:33:56.100
The subsection doesn't require anything to be built.

02:33:56.100 --> 02:34:02.100
So I would obviously ask that you vote down the amendment. Thank you.

02:34:02.100 --> 02:34:08.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the amendment? Seeing none, Representative, you may close on your motion.

02:34:08.100 --> 02:34:14.100
I guess I would just argue that if this bill truly does not require construction,

02:34:14.100 --> 02:34:16.100
I think it should have come out and said that.

02:34:16.100 --> 02:34:22.100
And if that's so, then it won't hurt for you to vote yes on my amendment, because we'll make sure that our municipalities are school districts,

02:34:22.100 --> 02:34:34.100
our colleges, our community colleges are not charged any undue costs associated with any construction as a result of this bill.

02:34:34.100 --> 02:34:36.100
I close.

02:34:36.100 --> 02:34:42.100
You've heard the motion by Representative McDonald to amend all those in favor signify by saying aye.

02:34:43.100 --> 02:34:45.100
All opposed nay.

02:34:45.100 --> 02:34:48.100
Nays appear to have it.

02:34:48.100 --> 02:34:53.100
The vision has been called. The clerk will open the roll and you may vote.

02:34:56.100 --> 02:34:59.100
Seeing 15 hands, the roll call is in order.

02:35:12.100 --> 02:35:32.100
As every member had the opportunity to vote.

02:35:32.100 --> 02:35:36.100
The clerk will close the roll and take the tally.

02:35:36.100 --> 02:35:40.100
Does any member wish to change their vote?

02:35:41.100 --> 02:35:49.100
Seeing none with 36 voting in favor and 86 against the motion fails. Representative McDonald, you still have the floor.

02:35:49.100 --> 02:35:51.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair.

02:35:51.100 --> 02:35:56.100
This is the first amendment I've done without messing up. So thanks for sticking with me on that.

02:35:56.100 --> 02:35:59.100
I can't can't argue with you.

02:35:59.100 --> 02:36:07.100
You know, it's pretty clear that the writing is on the wall for this and and I just want to give you all a last a last chance to.

02:36:07.100 --> 02:36:10.100
Consider the choice you're making here.

02:36:10.100 --> 02:36:13.100
The choice you're sending.

02:36:13.100 --> 02:36:16.100
The choice you make sends a message to Kansans.

02:36:16.100 --> 02:36:20.100
Do you focus on issues that.

02:36:20.100 --> 02:36:25.100
Add to division that vilify people.

02:36:25.100 --> 02:36:29.100
That stereotype people who are different than you.

02:36:29.100 --> 02:36:34.100
You do not have to understand something to be compassionate about it.

02:36:35.100 --> 02:36:43.100
This is your chance to take government out of people's bedrooms out of their bathrooms out of their business.

02:36:43.100 --> 02:36:48.100
My momma said you need to worry about yourself.

02:36:48.100 --> 02:36:54.100
My mom said don't go trying to solve problems that aren't even real.

02:36:54.100 --> 02:36:59.100
I just want to remind you also that we're soon going to be on the world stage.

02:36:59.100 --> 02:37:03.100
With the World Cup coming Kansas is going to be in the spotlight.

02:37:03.100 --> 02:37:12.100
Provisions like these are going to send signals to companies that Kansas focuses more on unnecessary and divisive issues in language.

02:37:12.100 --> 02:37:21.100
Then becoming a welcoming place where people want to visit, live, work and play.

02:37:21.100 --> 02:37:24.100
In Kansas we try to be good neighbors.

02:37:24.100 --> 02:37:29.100
We try to take care of each other.

02:37:29.100 --> 02:37:33.100
I understand that compassion is a short supply sometimes.

02:37:33.100 --> 02:37:37.100
But I would just like to appeal to you and your humanity.

02:37:37.100 --> 02:37:39.100
This is not necessary.

02:37:39.100 --> 02:37:45.100
And for my friends across the aisle, I'm guessing this is going to pass, but you don't have to be complicit.

02:37:45.100 --> 02:37:49.100
Please do the right thing and vote no on this bill. Thank you.

02:37:50.100 --> 02:38:04.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the bill? The chair recognizes represent Stog still.

02:38:04.100 --> 02:38:07.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair.

02:38:07.100 --> 02:38:15.100
Despite the protest stations of the peace loving leadership in the Department of War.

02:38:15.100 --> 02:38:25.100
We've had many and continue to have many transgender men and women serving patriotically and honorably in the United States military.

02:38:25.100 --> 02:38:42.100
In light of that, I have a few questions for the carrier of the bill.

02:38:42.100 --> 02:38:52.100
I apologize for having to ask these questions here. I would have preferred to have done it in committee before your committee, but that wasn't afforded me the opportunity.

02:38:52.100 --> 02:39:00.100
First question I had, how would this impact military bases in Kansas?

02:39:00.100 --> 02:39:02.100
They're federal.

02:39:02.100 --> 02:39:06.100
And so this is a state law, right?

02:39:06.100 --> 02:39:11.100
So they I don't believe we have jurisdiction over our federal buildings.

02:39:11.100 --> 02:39:21.100
Do they have the same laws about transgender bathrooms on at Fort Riley and Leavenworth or spaces and so on that we would have in the rest of the state?

02:39:21.100 --> 02:39:22.100
I don't know.

02:39:22.100 --> 02:39:35.100
So transgender military personnel would have to have one set of laws that they have to follow on our military bases and another set of laws possibly as they go off base.

02:39:35.100 --> 02:39:38.100
And also the same in public buildings.

02:39:38.100 --> 02:39:41.100
Because that's different. This is only about government buildings.

02:39:41.100 --> 02:39:46.100
Do we have do we have any state buildings on any of our military bases?

02:39:46.100 --> 02:39:47.100
I don't know.

02:39:47.100 --> 02:39:53.100
That would have been something I would have liked to know. How about the Kansas National Guard? How does this apply to them?

02:39:53.100 --> 02:39:55.100
Same, I suppose.

02:39:55.100 --> 02:39:56.100
I don't know.

02:39:56.100 --> 02:39:58.100
Federalized at some point.

02:39:58.100 --> 02:40:01.100
And at some point we would talk about it, I suppose.

02:40:02.100 --> 02:40:11.100
And how about military recruiting facilities that are being used in state office buildings and so on?

02:40:13.100 --> 02:40:16.100
Are the buildings owned by the government?

02:40:16.100 --> 02:40:17.100
Awesome.

02:40:17.100 --> 02:40:21.100
So, but there are federal employees, federal military employees.

02:40:21.100 --> 02:40:26.100
Yeah. So this bill is about government buildings that are owned or leased by the government.

02:40:26.100 --> 02:40:43.100
Okay. So if they're recruiting officers and so on on these facilities and they're in a state building, they have to ignore the laws that govern them when there are military bases because of Kansas laws while they're in recruiting kids to join the military.

02:40:43.100 --> 02:40:48.100
I think they just have to look at what's on the door and accordingly do that.

02:40:48.100 --> 02:40:58.100
What about tourists traveling across Kansas, not knowing the laws? What happens if they're stopped or what happens if somebody confronts them in a bathroom?

02:40:58.100 --> 02:41:11.100
I know like when you're coming in from Colorado, it's legal to have recreational marijuana there, but we've had Coloradans stopped in Kansas for not following Kansas law.

02:41:11.100 --> 02:41:21.100
Would tourists be subject to arrest and prosecution if they didn't follow Kansas law on our bathrooms?

02:41:21.100 --> 02:41:26.100
So this is not about a crime.

02:41:26.100 --> 02:41:33.100
This is about a complaint could be filed to the government entity and it would be investigated.

02:41:33.100 --> 02:41:39.100
The government entity would actually have three days to cure the violation if it was under them.

02:41:39.100 --> 02:41:45.100
Otherwise, a complaint could be made. There is a private cause of action, but at the most, it's a misdemeanor.

02:41:45.100 --> 02:41:48.100
I mean, we're not talking about someone being arrested.

02:41:48.100 --> 02:41:56.100
There's a lot of, I mean, in the bill, it goes into great detail what the enforcement is.

02:41:56.100 --> 02:41:58.100
Okay.

02:41:58.100 --> 02:42:02.100
Thank you. Appreciate it.

02:42:02.100 --> 02:42:16.100
My constituents will hate this bill and they will certainly even more hate the process that it's gone through to get here before the members of this House of Representatives.

02:42:17.100 --> 02:42:22.100
My constituents overwhelmingly support LGBTQ rights.

02:42:22.100 --> 02:42:34.100
And they also expect to have the right to come to Topeka and express their opinion either verbally or in writing. They had neither.

02:42:34.100 --> 02:42:45.100
Talk about, I guarantee you, I have never voted on a bill that has a fiscal note that reads blank.

02:42:45.100 --> 02:42:55.100
And you can say, well, this is not going to cost anything. This is going to be cheap. This isn't going to be expensive. This isn't going to cause problems in our construction industry or anything.

02:42:55.100 --> 02:42:59.100
Well, if that's the case, why did they leave it blank?

02:42:59.100 --> 02:43:04.100
That leaves it open for interpretation. That leaves it open for anything.

02:43:04.100 --> 02:43:17.100
I think we are absolutely beyond our scope when we vote for a bill that has the potential for millions of dollars potentially in building costs and so on.

02:43:17.100 --> 02:43:27.100
And we vote for it because the fiscal note is blank.

02:43:27.100 --> 02:43:36.100
This is money and time that we should be spending on debating property tax reduction.

02:43:36.100 --> 02:43:49.100
This is time and money we should be spending on how do we fund special education in the state of Kansas, not this.

02:43:49.100 --> 02:44:04.100
This is the kind of legislation that will hurt our state, our counties, our cities, our school districts, our colleges and our universities and the ability to to attract students to Kansas and businesses to Kansas.

02:44:04.100 --> 02:44:10.100
I can tell you after having worked with the high tech industries for eight years in this state and in Missouri.

02:44:11.100 --> 02:44:16.100
This is the kind of legislation that discourages those kinds of companies from coming to Kansas.

02:44:16.100 --> 02:44:32.100
And if you don't believe me, call some legislators in North Carolina and see how well that worked out for them when they started doing this kind of legislation.

02:44:32.100 --> 02:44:43.100
My constituents sent me up here to talk about property taxes to talk about public education to talk about public health. How do we make life speed life better for people in Kansas.

02:44:43.100 --> 02:44:50.100
Not to discriminate against certain people in the state of Kansas who have done absolutely nothing wrong.

02:44:51.100 --> 02:45:07.100
And I guarantee you my constituents will remember this bill and remember this process from now right up through November 3, 2026.

02:45:07.100 --> 02:45:15.100
Thank you, Mr. Speaker.

02:45:15.100 --> 02:45:18.100
Any other, does any other member wish to speak the bill?

02:45:18.100 --> 02:45:42.100
The chair recognizes representative Schlingen Seepen.

02:45:42.100 --> 02:45:45.100
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

02:45:45.100 --> 02:45:59.100
Colleagues, I rise because once again, we have a bill that my mind is part of the politics of polarization.

02:45:59.100 --> 02:46:04.100
Politics of polarization, I think serves only one point.

02:46:04.100 --> 02:46:10.100
We don't want to get things done that people need to have done because of ideological clashes.

02:46:10.100 --> 02:46:29.100
You create all kinds of problems that don't exist in order to prey on the fears of people about all kinds of things rather than trying to allay fears, create unity and deal with policy issues that really matter.

02:46:29.100 --> 02:46:47.100
When it comes to LGBTQIA rights and to those of our fellow trans citizens, I'd like to say that I'm very proud of my congregation, the first congregational church in Topeka, that has visited this issue in its own building,

02:46:47.100 --> 02:46:56.100
and that has removed all gender designations from the doors because we don't need them.

02:46:57.100 --> 02:47:08.100
There's no one feels threatened because we try to be an inclusive church and we try to understand each other, and we work very hard at trying to understand each other.

02:47:08.100 --> 02:47:23.100
Because otherwise, what happens is that we get bathroom bills and we go back to times when bathrooms and bigotry seem to have not only been a literative, but reality.

02:47:24.100 --> 02:47:30.100
Why do we think that putting targets on people's backs is okay?

02:47:30.100 --> 02:47:47.100
If we understood the reasons that we put trends, people in the crosshairs of our policy constantly in this body, at least over the four years I've been here, in one form or another, we realized why we really did that, we wouldn't be bringing bills like this.

02:47:47.100 --> 02:47:56.100
But either we don't realize it, that what we're really responding to are the fears we've created and maybe even believe in ourselves.

02:47:56.100 --> 02:48:01.100
In fact, the evidence tells a very different story.

02:48:01.100 --> 02:48:15.100
Trans-Cansans are not a threat to anyone, but there are a lot of people in Cansans who will be embolded by this kind of legislation and other kinds of legislation to harass transgender people, and it's happening already in our state.

02:48:15.100 --> 02:48:20.100
My district stands firmly behind the trans community.

02:48:20.100 --> 02:48:23.100
My church stands behind the trans community.

02:48:23.100 --> 02:48:25.100
I know it takes work to get to that place.

02:48:25.100 --> 02:48:29.100
I've pastored my church for 25 years now, going on 26.

02:48:29.100 --> 02:48:33.100
And we worked very, very hard to be an inclusive church.

02:48:33.100 --> 02:48:38.100
And it wasn't just saying we're inclusive and putting a sticker on the door that says everybody's welcome.

02:48:38.100 --> 02:48:54.100
It was a process of everybody from age 96 down to five years old learning what we're talking about and understanding who that affects in our community and why we should care about each other anyway.

02:48:54.100 --> 02:48:59.100
Because we don't believe that people are labels.

02:48:59.100 --> 02:49:02.100
We believe that people are people.

02:49:02.100 --> 02:49:12.100
Building relationships is the best way to combat this kind of generalized bigotry.

02:49:12.100 --> 02:49:16.100
I asked myself the question with regard to this bill.

02:49:16.100 --> 02:49:38.100
What happens when the hairy at birth female that my distinguished colleague from Sedgwick spoke to earlier is in the women's bathroom and someone files a complaint because they feel unsafe?

02:49:38.100 --> 02:49:43.100
Well, what's going to happen then is a process that could lead to significant fines.

02:49:43.100 --> 02:49:52.100
And it's going to create a witch hunt, which is what all kinds of motivated bills like this ultimately lead to.

02:49:52.100 --> 02:50:05.100
They put targets on people's backs, verbal targets, and then people out there who aren't as discerning.

02:50:05.100 --> 02:50:11.100
Maybe you don't have the kind of filters that we expect the civilized society feel they can take matters into their own hands.

02:50:11.100 --> 02:50:28.100
So, with that I have an amendment.

02:50:28.100 --> 02:50:38.100
It appears short the clerk will read.

02:50:38.100 --> 02:50:56.100
I move to amend house substitute for SB 244 on page four in line 16 after H by inserting it is a violation of this section to make a false complaint either to the attorney general under subsection E or to a governing body or chief administrative officer under subsection G.

02:50:56.100 --> 02:51:03.100
Any person who commits a violation under this subsection shall be subject to the notice provision under subsection G1.

02:51:03.100 --> 02:51:10.100
Any person who commits a second violation of this subsection shall be subject to the civil penalty provisions of subsection G2.

02:51:10.100 --> 02:51:17.100
Any person who commits a third or subsequent violation of this subsection shall be guilty of a class B misdemeanor.

02:51:17.100 --> 02:51:23.100
And by redesignating subsections paragraphs sub paragraphs and clauses accordingly.

02:51:23.100 --> 02:51:42.100
Representative, do you wish to explain your amendment? Yes, thank you, Mr. Chairman. In short, what this means is that if someone makes a false report, they are subject to the same penalties that are being imposed on those who would be found in the wrong bathrooms.

02:51:42.100 --> 02:51:56.100
This basically creates a situation in which people who think that they are part of some kind of posse to solve problems, nobody wants solved that they're going to have to pay a penalty for doing so.

02:51:56.100 --> 02:51:59.100
And with that, I move my amendment.

02:51:59.100 --> 02:52:04.100
Does anybody wish to speak to the amendment?

02:52:04.100 --> 02:52:06.100
Any other questions.

02:52:06.100 --> 02:52:19.100
The chair recognizes Representative Lewis.

02:52:20.100 --> 02:52:25.100
So appreciate your proposed amendment.

02:52:25.100 --> 02:52:36.100
I'm wondering if you looked at whether or not we already have something in an all law for filing a false report with law enforcement.

02:52:36.100 --> 02:52:41.100
I don't know that we do, but I assume by the question that we probably do.

02:52:42.100 --> 02:52:52.100
Quite frankly, I don't, I would guess that we do, but I don't know either. That's why I'm asking if you check to know that. I don't know that. Okay. All right.

02:52:52.100 --> 02:52:55.100
Thank you. Thank you.

02:52:55.100 --> 02:53:01.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the amendment?

02:53:01.100 --> 02:53:08.100
The chair recognizes Representative Humphries.

02:53:09.100 --> 02:53:15.100
Thank you, Mr. Chairman. I would say this is an unfriendly amendment. Thank you.

02:53:15.100 --> 02:53:23.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the amendment? Seeing none, Representative Schlingen, you may close.

02:53:23.100 --> 02:53:28.100
Well, I think an unfriendly amendment belongs with an unfriendly bill.

02:53:28.100 --> 02:53:31.100
And with that, I close.

02:53:31.100 --> 02:53:38.100
You've heard the motion by Representative Schlingen seeping to amend all those in favor signify by saying aye.

02:53:38.100 --> 02:53:41.100
I'll oppose nay.

02:53:41.100 --> 02:53:44.100
Nays appear to have it.

02:53:44.100 --> 02:53:51.100
Division has been called. The clerk will open the roll and you may vote.

02:53:51.100 --> 02:53:55.100
Seeing 15 hands, the roll call is in order.

02:54:01.100 --> 02:54:22.100
As every member had the opportunity to vote.

02:54:22.100 --> 02:54:30.100
The clerk will close the roll and take the tally. Does any member wish to change their vote?

02:54:30.100 --> 02:54:37.100
Seeing none with 36 voting in favor and 81 against the motion fails. Representative Schlingen seeping, you still have the floor.

02:54:37.100 --> 02:54:44.100
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

02:54:44.100 --> 02:54:47.100
I just want to say that.

02:54:47.100 --> 02:54:51.100
I just find this very disappointing.

02:54:51.100 --> 02:54:58.100
They were here once again, putting people who are already very, very vulnerable in a more vulnerable position.

02:54:58.100 --> 02:55:01.100
It makes me sad.

02:55:01.100 --> 02:55:06.100
It makes me sad as a pastor, as a politician, as a human being.

02:55:06.100 --> 02:55:16.100
And all I can say is, if someone's ever near 17th and Collins, 1st Congregational Church has bathrooms for you.

02:55:16.100 --> 02:55:19.100
So, thank you, Mr. Chairman. Thank you.

02:55:19.100 --> 02:55:25.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the bill? The chair recognizes Representative Chuck Smith.

02:55:50.100 --> 02:55:55.100
Thanks, Mr. Chair.

02:55:55.100 --> 02:56:02.100
You know, as a geometry teacher, one of the things I thought my kids was listened.

02:56:02.100 --> 02:56:08.100
So, I've listened to everybody and took some notes on what I disagree with.

02:56:08.100 --> 02:56:14.100
First off, have you been to a Kansas City Chiefs game?

02:56:15.100 --> 02:56:20.100
I guarantee you, in the men's bathroom, there is no private space.

02:56:20.100 --> 02:56:27.100
I guarantee you, when they build that new stadium in the men's bathroom,

02:56:27.100 --> 02:56:33.100
I promise you there's not going to be a stall for everybody that needs to go to the bathroom.

02:56:33.100 --> 02:56:38.100
It's not going to be there.

02:56:38.100 --> 02:56:43.100
My friend said to me, I hope both sides can work together.

02:56:43.100 --> 02:56:50.100
Guys, if we can't agree on this, we can't work together on almost anything.

02:56:50.100 --> 02:56:53.100
This is pretty sad, I think.

02:56:53.100 --> 02:56:58.100
Cost?

02:56:58.100 --> 02:57:02.100
You know, we shouldn't have left the blank. We should have said zero.

02:57:02.100 --> 02:57:04.100
There's no cost.

02:57:04.100 --> 02:57:09.100
The men go to the men's bathroom, the women's go to the women's bathroom.

02:57:09.100 --> 02:57:17.100
And if it's a single unit, wherever it goes in, locks the door, nobody else can come in.

02:57:17.100 --> 02:57:20.100
That's easy.

02:57:20.100 --> 02:57:22.100
Driver's license.

02:57:22.100 --> 02:57:30.100
The only time I ever get my driver's license out is when I go to the airport.

02:57:30.100 --> 02:57:33.100
And they're not going to stop me from going through when they looked at it

02:57:33.100 --> 02:57:36.100
and say, yeah, let's you go ahead.

02:57:36.100 --> 02:57:39.100
They're not going to stop me.

02:57:39.100 --> 02:57:44.100
I heard one of my friends said the quality of life.

02:57:44.100 --> 02:57:50.100
I have three beautiful daughters and a beautiful girlfriend of 49 years.

02:57:50.100 --> 02:57:55.100
And I want to give them the quality of life also.

02:57:55.100 --> 02:58:00.100
And I tell you what, if they're in the bathroom and a man comes in,

02:58:00.100 --> 02:58:04.100
you have taken that away from them.

02:58:04.100 --> 02:58:12.100
They deserve that quality of life also.

02:58:12.100 --> 02:58:19.100
Like I say, it's, you know, they say we rush this bill.

02:58:19.100 --> 02:58:21.100
This is my eighth year.

02:58:21.100 --> 02:58:26.100
And I remember talking in some ways about it for all eight years.

02:58:26.100 --> 02:58:30.100
Maybe not exactly like this, but something about like this for eight years.

02:58:30.100 --> 02:58:32.100
That's not new.

02:58:32.100 --> 02:58:37.100
We didn't rush it.

02:58:37.100 --> 02:58:40.100
Please give my girls the quality of life they deserve.

02:58:40.100 --> 02:58:42.100
Thank you.

02:58:42.100 --> 02:58:44.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the bill?

02:58:44.100 --> 02:59:11.100
The chair recognizes representative Lewis for the second time.

02:59:11.100 --> 02:59:27.100
Mr. Chair, I have an amendment.

02:59:27.100 --> 02:59:29.100
The amendment is extremely short.

02:59:29.100 --> 02:59:35.100
The clerk shall read.

02:59:35.100 --> 02:59:37.100
It is a simple amendment.

02:59:37.100 --> 02:59:40.100
The bill as presented.

02:59:40.100 --> 03:00:00.100
Provides that notwithstanding the provisions of B through G, which require that the.

03:00:00.100 --> 03:00:08.100
Government entity designate bathrooms as one sex or the other multi.

03:00:08.100 --> 03:00:11.100
Multi occupancy.

03:00:11.100 --> 03:00:13.100
It goes through some exceptions.

03:00:13.100 --> 03:00:20.100
And one of those is that a child who is under 12 years of age may enter a multi occupancy

03:00:20.100 --> 03:00:24.100
private space designated for use only by individuals of the opposite sex.

03:00:24.100 --> 03:00:28.100
If accompanied by an individual caring for such child.

03:00:28.100 --> 03:00:41.100
And my amendment just moves that age from instead of the current 12 under 12 to under nine.

03:00:41.100 --> 03:00:43.100
Is there anybody wishing to speak to the amendment?

03:00:43.100 --> 03:00:52.100
The chair recognizes representative Carmichael.

03:00:52.100 --> 03:00:56.100
Mr. Chair, I rise in opposition to the amendment.

03:00:56.100 --> 03:01:06.100
The hallmark of a poorly prepared piece of legislation is a series of floor amendments.

03:01:06.100 --> 03:01:09.100
This bill was rushed into the committee.

03:01:09.100 --> 03:01:18.100
It wasn't given a full opportunity for consideration by the members of the committee because we literally didn't see it until.

03:01:18.100 --> 03:01:25.100
Moments. I mean, after the amendment made been made in committee, we never had a chance to really look at it.

03:01:25.100 --> 03:01:34.100
But surely the author of the amendment took time to look at what he was bringing to the house judiciary committee.

03:01:34.100 --> 03:01:36.100
But it would appear that mistakes were made.

03:01:36.100 --> 03:01:42.100
Omissions were made that were legislating on the fly.

03:01:42.100 --> 03:01:44.100
Citizens of Kansas.

03:01:44.100 --> 03:01:49.100
Is there better than a knee jerk bill? Thank you, Mr. Chair.

03:01:49.100 --> 03:01:55.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the amendment? The chair recognizes representative Michael.

03:01:55.100 --> 03:02:00.100
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

03:02:00.100 --> 03:02:03.100
I stand in strong opposition to this amendment.

03:02:03.100 --> 03:02:09.100
When I read this bill, I saw this language about children younger than 12 and I thought.

03:02:09.100 --> 03:02:12.100
Okay, at least somebody thought about that provision.

03:02:13.100 --> 03:02:16.100
Lowering that age to nine. It's incredibly worrisome.

03:02:16.100 --> 03:02:20.100
I have a son who is going to turn nine in three weeks.

03:02:20.100 --> 03:02:30.100
And so if this were to go into effect, that means when I take him to Alan Fieldhouse or the KU Stadium or wherever in three weeks,

03:02:30.100 --> 03:02:36.100
I just have to send him into the men's room unaccompanied if my husband is not with me.

03:02:36.100 --> 03:02:48.100
Whereas if we reject this amendment, I will still have the option when I feel like I want to escort him into the women's room to be able to do that until he turns 12.

03:02:48.100 --> 03:02:52.100
I strongly oppose this amendment.

03:02:52.100 --> 03:03:07.100
Is there any other member wishing to speak the amendment? The chair recognizes representative Poskin.

03:03:07.100 --> 03:03:12.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair, to the point that both of my previous colleagues made.

03:03:12.100 --> 03:03:16.100
If this part of the bill had been vetted properly, had a public hearing.

03:03:16.100 --> 03:03:26.100
Parents of all ages of children could have commented on that and would have been able to tell you what representative from the representative from Lawrence just did.

03:03:26.100 --> 03:03:31.100
That is too young and I'm the mom of seven and grand parent of.

03:03:31.100 --> 03:03:45.100
Or and a question to the bringer of the amendment. If he will stand for a question.

03:03:45.100 --> 03:03:52.100
Thank you represent welcome. So when I just said that a question popped in my head. What about grandparents?

03:03:53.100 --> 03:03:57.100
Well, when I take my child to.

03:03:57.100 --> 03:04:03.100
The stadium or to, you know, Alan Fieldhouse.

03:04:03.100 --> 03:04:11.100
And they're nine. Can I go in with them? Yes, if you're caring for such child hearing for such child. Thank you. Yeah, you're welcome. Okay.

03:04:11.100 --> 03:04:18.100
I still stand opposed to the amendment and we really should have vetted this and let parents tell their stories.

03:04:19.100 --> 03:04:29.100
Is there anybody else? Is there any other member wishing to speak to the amendment? The chair recognizes representative McDonald.

03:04:29.100 --> 03:04:34.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair. Would you be willing to stand for a question?

03:04:34.100 --> 03:04:36.100
Thank you so much.

03:04:36.100 --> 03:04:41.100
I stepped out to go to the restroom. So I may have missed when you said this. So if you'll indulge me. Sure.

03:04:41.100 --> 03:04:44.100
Exactly what problem would your amendment solve?

03:04:44.100 --> 03:05:06.100
Like what is what is the reason for you bring in the amendment? Well, the original bill said that if a child under the age of 12 is being cared for that the adult caregiver can take him or her into a space that's not designated for his biological sex.

03:05:06.100 --> 03:05:20.100
And we're moving that down to nine or under under nine. And I think that thinking was from talking to colleagues that, you know, the age at the age of nine.

03:05:20.100 --> 03:05:31.100
It's unlikely that somebody is going to need to be accompanied into a bathroom. And at that age, they become becoming aware of sexual differences.

03:05:31.100 --> 03:05:42.100
So to be clear, if someone is 10, but their grandparents are with them, they can't go to the bathroom with their grandparent if their grandparents the opposite sex. Yes, that's right.

03:05:42.100 --> 03:05:46.100
Okay, thank you.

03:05:46.100 --> 03:06:01.100
I was raised with sisters. And when when they said how to ultrasound and every time they said it's a boy, I was just freaking out a little bit because all we have in our family is girls and I had to figure out how to do this thing to be employment.

03:06:01.100 --> 03:06:17.100
And one of my biggest challenges was the bathroom situation. And I'm always so grateful to find a family bathroom where the whole family can get in there with a double stroller and, you know, all of that, all of the equipment, the diaper bags.

03:06:17.100 --> 03:06:25.100
But that's not always the case. And so I feel like this amendment makes this bill even more dangerous somehow.

03:06:25.100 --> 03:06:36.100
As the mother of two boys, they were there about 13 months apart. Very, very often I was juggling toddlers running, which, you know, different directions.

03:06:36.100 --> 03:06:46.100
And I can't imagine when I think back to my son, like I said, he grew his hair out when he was about six, eight. When he was nine, he looked like a girl. I'm just going to say it.

03:06:47.100 --> 03:07:03.100
When he was 10, he certainly looked like a girl. And because of the way I choose to raise my children, they're very aware of sexual differences. They know a lot about human reproduction because that's my right as a parent to make sure that they understand good touch, bad touch.

03:07:03.100 --> 03:07:20.100
Yeah, they're on the lookout for people who might be nefarious, but also it's intimidating for a grown adult to come into a bathroom and boss around a kid, even if they're 10 years old, 11 years old, 15 years old, 17 years old.

03:07:21.100 --> 03:07:34.100
This amendment is really dangerous, in my opinion. So I'm just strongly going to urge you to vote. No, even if you love the bill, this amendment really makes things more dangerous for our children. Thank you.

03:07:34.100 --> 03:07:39.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the amendment? The chair recognizes representative Martinez.

03:07:51.100 --> 03:08:03.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair. I'd just like to remind you guys of an incident in 1998, a young man by the name of Matthew Keechi. He was nine years old.

03:08:03.100 --> 03:08:15.100
But he went into a restroom in Oceanside Harbor in California. He was a third grader while his aunt waited outside, and he was murdered.

03:08:15.100 --> 03:08:22.100
I believe he's the one whose throat was slit when he was in there. Now, this was in a shelter, I believe, off the side of a highway.

03:08:22.100 --> 03:08:29.100
But these things can happen. You never know who's going to be in the bathroom when you're not there.

03:08:29.100 --> 03:08:41.100
My granddaughters, 13, 14, 15 years old, if it's an unfamiliar space, I go in the bathroom with them just to be safe. You can roll your eyes all you want.

03:08:41.100 --> 03:08:48.100
If it doesn't affect you, roll your eyes, laugh, whatever you want to do. This is serious.

03:08:48.100 --> 03:09:01.100
We need to think about the possibilities. A male going into a person who appears to be a male, going into a female bathroom is not a good thing.

03:09:01.100 --> 03:09:08.100
And it also opens the door for a real male to go into the bathroom. Same thing with a female bathroom. I mean, a male bathroom.

03:09:08.100 --> 03:09:15.100
There's nothing else can go in there, too. So we need to rethink what we're doing with some of this stuff in this bill.

03:09:15.100 --> 03:09:19.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair.

03:09:19.100 --> 03:09:27.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the amendment? The Chair recognizes Representative Humphries.

03:09:27.100 --> 03:09:34.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair. I think this amendment makes a lot of sense, and I would encourage you to vote yes.

03:09:34.100 --> 03:09:41.100
Does any other member wish to speak to the amendment? Seeing none, Representative Lewis, you may close on your motion.

03:09:41.100 --> 03:09:46.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair. I close.

03:09:46.100 --> 03:09:53.100
You've heard the motion by Representative Lewis to amend all those in favor, signified by saying aye.

03:09:53.100 --> 03:09:56.100
I'll oppose, nay.

03:09:56.100 --> 03:09:59.100
The ayes appear to have it.

03:09:59.100 --> 03:10:02.100
The ayes do have it. The motion carries.

03:10:02.100 --> 03:10:14.100
Representative Lewis, you stole the floor.

03:10:14.100 --> 03:10:19.100
So we've been out this for a while, and I'm going to try and be as succinct as possible.

03:10:19.100 --> 03:10:22.100
This bill really isn't that complicated.

03:10:22.100 --> 03:10:27.100
This bill simply codifies societal norms.

03:10:27.100 --> 03:10:30.100
That is what people expect.

03:10:30.100 --> 03:10:41.100
That public spaces used for private purposes will be single sex, and that a person's identification documents will accurately reflect their biological sex.

03:10:41.100 --> 03:10:48.100
That's just what people expect, and we're simply codifying that with this bill.

03:10:48.100 --> 03:10:54.100
The bill has too many, two primary purposes. One is privacy and public safety.

03:10:54.100 --> 03:11:03.100
With respect to bathrooms, but we're also talking, by the way, about more than bathrooms, we're talking about locker rooms, schools.

03:11:03.100 --> 03:11:11.100
We're talking not just about adults, but we're talking about children in these public private spaces as well.

03:11:11.100 --> 03:11:19.100
And I have heard from a number of constituents and others about concerns about both privacy,

03:11:19.100 --> 03:11:29.100
especially women privacy and safety inside these public spaces where men are allowed to come in and use the facilities at the same time.

03:11:29.100 --> 03:11:32.100
So both privacy and safety.

03:11:32.100 --> 03:11:41.100
With respect to safety, there's another concern that hasn't been raised, but we did learn about this in our hearing on the bill.

03:11:41.100 --> 03:11:53.100
That is in our in merge in situations where people come into say an emergency room, or they're in a car accident and they're incapacitated.

03:11:53.100 --> 03:12:08.100
And what the hospitalist that from Wesley testified to was medical professionals need to know immediately whether that person is biological male or female in order to make good medical decisions.

03:12:08.100 --> 03:12:23.100
And they will then in those circumstances rely heavily on what the driver's license says. So if the driver's license isn't accurate with respect to the biological sex of the person, it creates problems for the medical treatment.

03:12:23.100 --> 03:12:31.100
And that will affect, by the way, not only the people who are the trans people, but anybody.

03:12:32.100 --> 03:12:41.100
They need to have reliable information from the driver's license about the biological sex to provide them with the best medical treatment possible.

03:12:41.100 --> 03:12:47.100
The other concern that really hasn't been addressed is law enforcement IDs.

03:12:48.100 --> 03:13:05.100
What happens is when a police officer law enforcement officer stops somebody, they inevitably ask for the driver's license and what they then do is they go back into their squad car and whatever and they they send the information on the driver's license into the

03:13:06.100 --> 03:13:20.100
CIS database and they on based on that driver's license and the information in it, which would include their birth date, their name, and their gender or their sex.

03:13:20.100 --> 03:13:34.100
They made that that in I CIS database searches to determine whether, for instance, that individual has a warrant out for their arrest or has, you know, five felonies that they've been convicted of.

03:13:34.100 --> 03:13:41.100
It would be nice. It would be important for that law enforcement to know with whom he or she is dealing.

03:13:41.100 --> 03:13:45.100
So those are some of the concerns that I think.

03:13:45.100 --> 03:13:49.100
Undergird, if you would, this bill.

03:13:49.100 --> 03:13:52.100
And I think it's important that we keep those in mind.

03:13:52.100 --> 03:13:59.100
I also want to respond to some of the arguments that my friends from the other side of the aisle have been making.

03:14:00.100 --> 03:14:06.100
First of all, we've heard that the bill targets or discriminates is against transgenders. I reject that. Why?

03:14:06.100 --> 03:14:10.100
Everyone in this bill is treated just the same.

03:14:10.100 --> 03:14:21.100
It simply requires that everyone, whether they're a male who wants to be a female, female who wants to be a male, a male who wants to be a male, female who wants to be a male.

03:14:21.100 --> 03:14:30.100
It doesn't matter. They use the private space in public, multiple occupancy spaces of their biological sex.

03:14:30.100 --> 03:14:35.100
And it requires that they have identification documents that reflect the biological gender.

03:14:35.100 --> 03:14:48.100
Having the gender marker on the driver's license, as I say, reflects the biological gender of the person and can actually be helpful to the transgender population.

03:14:48.100 --> 03:14:56.100
We've also heard a lot about the cost of this. Look at our own building here.

03:14:56.100 --> 03:15:05.100
There's not going to be a cost involved in this building coming into compliance with this bill.

03:15:06.100 --> 03:15:19.100
We have genders designated multiple occupancy bathrooms, and we also have a family bathroom. There's not going to be any construction needed.

03:15:19.100 --> 03:15:32.100
And I would doubt that there are very many government buildings in this state that don't have the same designations right and left or female and male.

03:15:32.100 --> 03:15:47.100
The only difference is now there's going to be codification of that, and there's going to be enforcement of those sex distinctions, which are, as I said, really part, really part of just a societal norm.

03:15:47.100 --> 03:15:52.100
Let's see.

03:15:53.100 --> 03:16:10.100
There was some discussion of what's going to happen to people who don't know about the law when they come into Kansas. Well, I, I point out to you, you know, then marijuana laws were mentioned. We have marijuana distinctive different marijuana laws in neighboring states.

03:16:10.100 --> 03:16:22.100
And that's the responsibility of those who come into Kansas or drive to Colorado or to Missouri to know what those laws are when they enter into those states.

03:16:22.100 --> 03:16:35.100
The same way it'll be the responsibility of people to know that in this case. The other thing I would point out is the law actually does not hold them responsible in the first instance of violation.

03:16:35.100 --> 03:16:46.100
It gives them a warning. That's it. Warning. Nothing else. Their second violation. They're subject to a thousand dollar penalty. And it's only upon the third that there's any kind of criminal sanction.

03:16:46.100 --> 03:16:55.100
So I think this law does a good job of giving people notice of their obligations to comply with it.

03:16:55.100 --> 03:17:20.100
And there's also been discussion about how unfortunate it will be for the concern expressed is that a person who is a, let's see, a person who is a woman, but, but looks like a man will enter a woman's private space.

03:17:20.100 --> 03:17:36.100
Think about that. This bill will significantly reduce that problem because currently any man can enter any woman's bathroom without legal consequence.

03:17:36.100 --> 03:17:54.100
That is a concern that we have. And I'm not saying that we're going to eliminate that problem, but we are going to significantly reduce the problem through this bill. So those who on this side of the aisle who are concerned about somebody looking like a man being in a woman's bathroom

03:17:54.100 --> 03:18:02.100
should actually support this bill because it's significantly going to reduce the risk of that happening.

03:18:06.100 --> 03:18:32.100
As far as the cost goes. Once again, the, I just don't think that there's going to be very many public buildings that are going to have to be reconfit bathrooms reconfigured or new new bathrooms built because of this bill.

03:18:32.100 --> 03:18:47.100
So I don't think there's going to be great cost involved to these public entities. And they will have some time to take care of that under the bill. There's, if they're complained against, they have a cure.

03:18:47.100 --> 03:18:56.100
If the entity that owns building is complained against, they have a cure period in which to fix the problem.

03:18:56.100 --> 03:19:04.100
And that Mr. Chairman, I urge support of the bill. Thank you.

03:19:04.100 --> 03:19:14.100
The chair recognizes representative Sutton for an announcement.

03:19:14.100 --> 03:19:17.100
Thank you, Mr. Leader, Mr. Chairman.

03:19:17.100 --> 03:19:32.100
I know this is going to break hearts, but in order for no one to feel any angst as the time ticks, I will be canceling insurance committee today.

03:19:32.100 --> 03:19:44.100
I know, I know. We'll try to schedule a double one on another day. We'll try to get a Friday or something. But anyway, thank you. Thank you, Mr. Chair.

03:19:44.100 --> 03:19:52.100
The chair recognizes representative Bryce for an announcement.

03:19:52.100 --> 03:20:12.100
Thank you, Mr. Chair. Health and Human Services Committee will not be meeting today. We'll meet tomorrow at the regular time. Thank you.

03:20:13.100 --> 03:20:22.100
